Sahra martini is relieved to be back  with her college classmates in Berlin  the Syrian refugee an activist was  released on bail after spending more  than three months in a green prison Sara  was returning from lesbos in August when  police arrested her she had spent the  summer volunteering on the Greek island  with a pro refugee organization because  of her work helping migrants authorities  charged her with people smuggling money  laundering and espionage she now faces a  prison sentence of up to 25 years I  never regret anything I've done and  actually I knew deep inside that I've  done that because I wanted to help  people and that's give me motivation her  arrest wasn't the first time that Sarah  made headlines she caught the world's  attention in 2015 but for very different  reasons  she and her sister yo Sarah were making  the perilous sea crossing from Turkey to  lesbos when their dinghy began to take  on water and sink being experienced  swimmers they jumped overboard and  pulled the waterlogged boat to the shore  by doing so they saved the lives of the  18 people on board  they began a new life in Germany yo  syrup became a goodwill ambassador for  the United Nations and was on the  refugee team at the 2016 Rio Olympics so  I want a scholarship to study social  sciences in Berlin but kept on returning  to lesbos to help others I believe when  you fear something just go for it  so I fear the place that I landed in and  I wanted to be on this ground again  following sirs arrest her college  classmates campaigned for her release  organizing protests in Berlin Boston  Athens and other cities her defense team  is calling for greater protection for  humanitarian workers it is an ongoing  conversation right now has to recognize  that has to recognize the criterion of  financial benefit as an obligatory  requirement in order to in order to  establish the crime of migrant smuggling  you cannot have smugglers without  benefits and this is obvious you have to  exclude from sanctions people and  organizations that provide humanitarian  aid until such safeguards are in place  Greek authorities can claim that Sarah  was helping migrants enter the country  illegally she's currently waiting for  her case to go to trial she hopes it  won't go that far  but is determined to prove her innocence  and that saving lives is not a crime
 

